{
  "case_id": "2017-16-460",
  "document_hash": "34f29e8f31f3bf45d70ec9ce37e3d92629b71223428d86ed30dd267a244a30a9",
  "records": [
    {
      "attribution": "Petitioner",
      "case_id": "2017-16-460",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "34f29e8f31f3bf45d70ec9ce37e3d92629b71223428d86ed30dd267a244a30a9",
      "evidence_id": "ev-34f29e8f31f3-0000-0000",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 4,
        "start": 4
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Title 28 Section 1367(d) specifies that the period of limitations on a supplemental jurisdiction claim shall be tolled while the claim is pending in federal court and for a period of 30 days after it is dismissed. The question before the Court this morning is, what does it mean for a period of limitations to be tolled? The Court should hold that \"tolled\" means suspended, an interpretation that accords with the plain meaning of the word \"tolled.\" That is the definition given in Black's Law Dictionary, and that is the way \"tolled\" is used in every other statute that uses the word \"tolled,\" none of which would make any sense under Respondent's Heritage Reporting Corporation"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kennedy",
      "case_id": "2017-16-460",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "34f29e8f31f3bf45d70ec9ce37e3d92629b71223428d86ed30dd267a244a30a9",
      "evidence_id": "ev-34f29e8f31f3-0000-0001",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 5,
        "start": 5
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE KENNEDY: If that's the -- if that's the way the statute operates, it seems to me that the provision at the end which says the state can provide for a longer tolling period is generally un- -- unnecessary, if the -- if the -- if -- under your position, it would seem to be quite unnecessary for the state to have a longer tolling period."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Sotomayor",
      "case_id": "2017-16-460",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "34f29e8f31f3bf45d70ec9ce37e3d92629b71223428d86ed30dd267a244a30a9",
      "evidence_id": "ev-34f29e8f31f3-0000-0003",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 10,
        "start": 10
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE SOTOMAYOR: Well, my problem is that I look at statutory history; not legislative history, statutory history. And the statutory history is that the ALI report set forth a very clear grace period or -- or -\u00ad grace period."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "MR. UNIKOWSKY",
      "case_id": "2017-16-460",
      "confidence": 0.95,
      "document_hash": "34f29e8f31f3bf45d70ec9ce37e3d92629b71223428d86ed30dd267a244a30a9",
      "evidence_id": "ev-34f29e8f31f3-0001-0000",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 24,
        "start": 24
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. UNIKOWSKY: Well, I think that statutes of limitations reflect a balance. And this -- and as this Court has said many times, it's true that one purpose is to protect defendants, and there's another purpose, to give plaintiffs a sufficiently long time to sue."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Breyer",
      "case_id": "2017-16-460",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "34f29e8f31f3bf45d70ec9ce37e3d92629b71223428d86ed30dd267a244a30a9",
      "evidence_id": "ev-34f29e8f31f3-0002-0001",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 46,
        "start": 46
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE BREYER: But is it -- well, look, aren't there many statutes, or I don't know how many, but isn't it somewhat normal, the federal government does say the thing is tolled, the state law is tolled while it's pending? Are there no other statutes like that where it just says the state law is pending -\u00ad is tolled while your federal suit is pending?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Breyer",
      "case_id": "2017-16-460",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "34f29e8f31f3bf45d70ec9ce37e3d92629b71223428d86ed30dd267a244a30a9",
      "evidence_id": "ev-34f29e8f31f3-0002-0007",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 47,
        "start": 47
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE BREYER: So, in other words, the -- the federal government in your view has the constitutional power to, area-by-area, to say we will toll the statute of limitations, i.e., suspend it? But it doesn't have the power to say it across the board. Is that your view?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kagan",
      "case_id": "2017-16-460",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "34f29e8f31f3bf45d70ec9ce37e3d92629b71223428d86ed30dd267a244a30a9",
      "evidence_id": "ev-34f29e8f31f3-0002-0003",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 52,
        "start": 52
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE KAGAN: Well, here, how about this? How about -- Ms. AliKhan, I mean, maybe one thing that -- that Justice Breyer should read is Jinks, where the Court already upheld Congress's authority to pass 1367(d) under the necessary and proper clause. And in doing so, it relied on an earlier decision of this Court, which upheld a federal provision that tolled state statutes of limitations by means of stop-clock suspension."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Adam G. Unikowski",
      "case_id": "2017-16-460",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "34f29e8f31f3bf45d70ec9ce37e3d92629b71223428d86ed30dd267a244a30a9",
      "evidence_id": "ev-34f29e8f31f3-0002-0006",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 60,
        "start": 60
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. UNIKOWSKY: Thank you, Mr. Chief Justice. I'd like to make just two rebuttal points, one about the plain text of this statute and one about the federalism considerations raised by Respondent."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Gorsuch",
      "case_id": "2017-16-460",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "34f29e8f31f3bf45d70ec9ce37e3d92629b71223428d86ed30dd267a244a30a9",
      "evidence_id": "ev-34f29e8f31f3-0003-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 66,
        "start": 66
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE GORSUCH: -- so where -- where do we draw the constitutional -- where would you have us draw that constitutional line?"
    },
    {
